 2. Americans&#x27; perplexing job market views 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Federal Reserve Bank of New York; Chart: Neil Irwin/Axios Americans are as pessimistic about the overall job market as they have been since the depths of the pandemic. But they also think that they, personally, will be just fine.

 The big picture: That is the perplexing combination of views displayed in the New York Fed's latest Survey of Consumer Expectations , which matches other survey-based evidence that Americans are doing well individually but are deeply negative about the overall economic outlook.

 By the numbers: The average survey respondent put the odds that the unemployment rate will be higher a year from now at 44.4% in August — the highest since April 2020 and up 1.6 percentage points from July.

 Yet survey respondents' average perceived odds of losing their own job in the next 12 months decreased by a 0.4 percentage point, to 13.8%, the lowest since February. Perceived odds of quitting their job voluntarily increased as well, by a 0.9 percentage point — a sign of confidence in respondents' personal job prospects. State of play: Labor market data has firmed up so far this year, with the unemployment rate at 4.1% the last two months after ending last year at 4.4%. That firming is reflected in people's perceptions of their personal job prospects, but not in their macro views.

 Zoom in: This curious mix of views aligns with other public opinion data.

 The Conference Board's consumer confidence index, for example, showed a July surge in the "present situation index" of current conditions, which was up 6.8 points, to 121.2. Meanwhile, the expectations index — consumers' outlook for income, business and the job market — fell 5.8 points, to 68.2. Between the lines: Survey responses about economic questions increasingly seem to bring sour responses that reflect broadly negative attitudes about the direction of the world.

 When you narrow the question to ask about peoples' immediate economic circumstances, the responses get a lot more favorable. 
 
